# CHANGELOG

## [1.1] - 2026-07-09

This release includes fixes to both the 5Q and Steane codepacks, the addition of a Surface-17/13/10 codepack, statevector performance upgrades, HDF5 serialization, upgraded documentation, alongside smaller bugfixes, refactors, and improvements.

### New
- **Surface-17/13/10 codepack (#39, #94, @sserita):** Implements a codepack for the surface code. Surface-17 (9 data, 8 auxiliary) is the default, but Surface-13 (only 4 auxiliary, reuse between X and Z checks) and Surface-10 (only 1 auxiliary, fully serialized stabilizer checks) are available via the `"layout"` keyword. The Surface-17/13/10 codepack uses `pyMatching` to implement a standard minimum-weight perfect matching (MWPM) decoder for use with a (non-adaptive) 3-round syndrome extraction.
- **`loqs[pymatching]` (#94, @sserita):** There is now an optional `"[pymatching]"` dependency when installing LoQS (required for Surface-17/13/10 codepack use). This is also installed if using `"[backends]"`.
- **Support for multiple logical qubits (#77, #93, @sserita):** Several minor changes have been made to facilitate multiple logical qubits. The most prominent is the addition of a `QECCodePatch.data` dict, enabling each patch to track their own syndrome, etc. This dict was also exposed as a data source with the param priority `"patch_data"`, checked after the `Instruction` but before the `QuantumProgram`.
- **HDF5 serialization (#71, #80, #83, @sserita):** The default serialization is now the HDF5 binary format rather than JSON (although JSON is still available). This includes a complete refactor of the `Serialization` class, and comes with some additional serialization improvements:
  - **Block checkpointing (#68):** Shots can now be checkpointed in batches rather than all at once or one by one. This is only performant for HDF5, as it relies on being able to merge checkpoints in-place rather than doing a full load/save that JSON requires.
  - **Improved caching (#83):** An improved caching system allows serialization to write each object only once, and then reference it when needed. This system differentiates between objects that are actually the same instance and objects that would serialize to the same content; the former are deserialized to point to the same object, while the latter use the same information but deserialize to copies.
  - Performance (both size and compute) is still being improved. Caching results in considerably reduced file size in some cases, but has a non-trivial computational cost.
- **Kraus operator representation checks (#65, @rileyjmurray):** The matrices passed to the Kraus representation type can now be checked to confirm that they satisfy the completeness relation.

### Changed
- **Performance improvements for `NumpyStatevectorQuantumState` (#63, #94, @kmrudin): Lazy evaluation of non-cacheable Kraus coefficients and a shift from `einsum` to `matmul` calls has increased the performance of the statevector backend by a (projected) factor of 5x to 20x speedups under normal circumstances.
- **`Serializable` refactor (#70, #71, #80, #83, @sserita):** Enabling HDF5 and JSON serialization required a major refactor of `Serializable`. While most of these changes are internal, we document the most visible here:
  - **`__hash__()` renamed to `serial_hash()` (#70, @rileyjmurray):** Same functionality but renamed as it relies on mutable data and is not suitable for an actual hashing function.
  - **Introduction of `_SERIALIZE_ATTRS` and `_SERIALIZE_ATTRS_MAP`:** Provides a general way to serialize LoQS objects. `Serialization`-derived objects no longer need to define their own encoding/decoding functions (but still can for non-standard processing).
  - **`to_serialization`/`from_serialization` renamed to `encode`/`decode`**
  - **Format-specific code migrated to `JSONEncoder/HDF5Encoder`**
- **Documentation upgrades (#54, #88, @sserita):** Documentation has moved to a custom two-stage MkDocs workflow. The API reference is autogenerated but features more information, formatting, and linkage than standard MkDocs implementations. A short-course set of tutorials has been added to the documentation. *This is still an area of active improvement. Please make GitHub issues for any problems you run into!*
- **`STIMPhysicalCircuit` refactor (#86, @rileyjmurray):** The monolithic `STIMPhysicalCircuit.__init__()` has been split into more digestible functions. No behavior changes.
- **Various repository improvements (#85, #84, #91, @sserita and @kmrudin):** Working coverage badge via Coveralls, improved GitHub Actions workflows, and README updates.

### Fixed
- **5Q codepack H permutation (#60, @sserita):** Fixes a mismatch between Pauli frame (*permuted*) and recorded syndrome (*not permuted*) data after the patch permutation for H. Both are now permuted and therefore internally consistent.
- **Steane codepack (#92, @kmrudin):** Applies a similar permutation fix as the 5Q codepack (#60). Additionally fixes a double-counting issue with the classical syndrome in logical measurement.


**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/sandialabs/LoQS/compare/v1.0...v1.1

## [1.0] - 2026-03-06

This marks the first public release of LoQS.
This includes the core LoQS API surrounding how to build user-defined functions into Instructions,
how to bundle these up into QECCodes, how to run QuantumPrograms, and use the resulting History object.

The following backends are supported:
- Circuit: PyGSTi, STIM, and native Python lists
- Model: PyGSTi and native Python dicts
- State: QuantumSim (density matrix), STIM (stabilizer), and native NumPy (statevector)

The following gate representations are supported:
- Unitary matrices
- Pauli-transfer matrices
- Superoperators in QuantumSim's 0xy1 basis
- STIM strings
- A list of STIM strings to apply probabilistically
- Kraus operators

The following measurement/instrument representations are supported:
- Perfect Z basis measurement (with or without reset)
- Perfect Z basis measurement (with or without reset) sandwiched by noisy gates
- PyGSTi-like instruments, i.e. dicts of outcomes to gates that should be applied

Other preliminary features include parallelization via Dask, JSON serialization,
and object display via tkinter.
Test coverage is moderate (~70%), and documentation covers most LoQS features and
is mostly up-to-date.